{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"9c3e7d28f211fd21","name":"card_event_sally","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/events/card_event_sally","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SALLY","text":"Target unit is pinned if a tank, suppressed if an infantry, destroyed if an artillery and retreats if an air unit.","flavorText":"During WWII, French aircraft played a limited but determined role in ground attack missions, particularly during the Battle of France in 1940. The most notable was the Bréguet Br.693, a twin-engine assault aircraft designed specifically for low-level attacks against enemy troops and armor. Entering service only shortly before the German invasion, around 200 units were built. Armed with machine guns, cannons and bombs, the Br.693 was used in desperate attempts to slow down the German advance. Despite their courage, crews faced intense anti-aircraft fire and high losses. Other aircraft, such as the Loire-Nieuport LN.411 and the Potez 633, also saw brief service in ground attack roles, though in smaller numbers. Many missions had to be improvised due to limited aircraft and poor coordination between air and ground forces. The French Air Forces also suffered from insufficient standardization and shortages of spare parts for their many different types of aircraft. After the armistice, a number of surviving planes were used by the Vichy regime or repurposed by Germany. Later in the war, Free French pilots flew Allied aircraft in North Africa and Europe.","faction":"France","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":0,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_sally","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_sally_title","SourceString":"SALLY","LocalizedString":"SALLY"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::France","Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_sally_text","SourceString":"Target unit is pinned if a tank, suppressed if an infantry, destroyed if an artillery and retreats if an air unit.","LocalizedString":"Target unit is pinned if a tank, suppressed if an infantry, destroyed if an artillery and retreats if an air unit."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_sally_history","SourceString":"During WWII, French aircraft played a limited but determined role in ground attack missions, particularly during the Battle of France in 1940. The most notable was the Bréguet Br.693, a twin-engine assault aircraft designed specifically for low-level attacks against enemy troops and armor. Entering service only shortly before the German invasion, around 200 units were built. Armed with machine guns, cannons and bombs, the Br.693 was used in desperate attempts to slow down the German advance. Despite their courage, crews faced intense anti-aircraft fire and high losses. Other aircraft, such as the Loire-Nieuport LN.411 and the Potez 633, also saw brief service in ground attack roles, though in smaller numbers. Many missions had to be improvised due to limited aircraft and poor coordination between air and ground forces. The French Air Forces also suffered from insufficient standardization and shortages of spare parts for their many different types of aircraft. After the armistice, a number of surviving planes were used by the Vichy regime or repurposed by Germany. Later in the war, Free French pilots flew Allied aircraft in North Africa and Europe.","LocalizedString":"During WWII, French aircraft played a limited but determined role in ground attack missions, particularly during the Battle of France in 1940. The most notable was the Bréguet Br.693, a twin-engine assault aircraft designed specifically for low-level attacks against enemy troops and armor. Entering service only shortly before the German invasion, around 200 units were built. Armed with machine guns, cannons and bombs, the Br.693 was used in desperate attempts to slow down the German advance. Despite their courage, crews faced intense anti-aircraft fire and high losses. Other aircraft, such as the Loire-Nieuport LN.411 and the Potez 633, also saw brief service in ground attack roles, though in smaller numbers. Many missions had to be improvised due to limited aircraft and poor coordination between air and ground forces. The French Air Forces also suffered from insufficient standardization and shortages of spare parts for their many different types of aircraft. After the armistice, a number of surviving planes were used by the Vichy regime or repurposed by Germany. Later in the war, Free French pilots flew Allied aircraft in North Africa and Europe."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","extraHelpTextBubbleTypes":["EHelpBubbleTypes::Pin","EHelpBubbleTypes::Suppress","EHelpBubbleTypes::Retreat"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/TACTICAL_AIRSTRIKE_17.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SALLY.SALLY","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":383.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_sally_photo","SourceString":"A French Potez 63.11 in North Africa. January 1943.","LocalizedString":"A French Potez 63.11 in North Africa. January 1943."},"OnPlayedFromHandVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_OrderDiveBomber.BP_OrderDiveBomber_C","SubPathString":""},"isRemoval":true},"variables":[{"name":"tmpCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_sally_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out"}],"pure":false,"event":true,"flag